#let abstract-text = [
  A renderer turns a scene into an image. A differentiable renderer also says how the image would change if the scene did. With path replay, that answer is correct only if the backward pass regenerates the paths the forward pass drew. We describe irys, an open-source spectral path tracer in Rust for the scenes we design (instanced CAD meshes, spectral metals, robots in rooms), whose replay reproduces the forward pass bit for bit.

  Its differentiable core is path replay backpropagation on a GPU wavefront. We write each material once as a graph in the tang tensor compiler, and tang lowers it both to GPU kernels and to an f64 CPU reference. An integer random-number generator, fixed-order reductions and a ray-only BVH walk make the replay exact on #V("gpu.replay_lanes") of #V("gpu.replay_lanes") lanes, and two runs give identical gradients. GPU f32 gradients agree with the f64 reference to #E("gpu.dtheta_max"). irys also computes pose gradients through instanced CAD meshes (primary edges, an interior term with attached frames, and secondary edges that move shadows and reflections) and a per-vertex image gradient that the vcad BRep kernel pulls back to the dimensions of a part.

  We check the GPU's f32 gradients against the f64 tier, the edge terms against central differences with common random numbers, and the shadows a moving part casts against the same differences with and without the secondary term. Pulled back through vcad's BRep kernel, irys's gradient with respect to a part's dimension agrees with an end-to-end render finite difference to #fx(100 * V("brep.e2e_rel"), 1)%. irys also differentiates a diffuse surface's spectral albedo and each Gaussian splat's colour and opacity, under the sky, a sun and area emitters, and the GPU tier runs on Vulkan as well as Metal. The remaining limits are that replay is bit-exact per device and driver, pose parameters are rigid, and the secondary-edge term runs on the CPU only. Three checks fall short, and we report them: edge sampling is #F("edge.box_rel1", 1)% high on one pose parameter of a box, the secondary term passes its check at 64 seeds but not at 256, and the end-to-end CAD finite difference agrees to its step dependence but not to the pullback's standard error. A companion paper covers inverse problems built on irys, starting with editable CAD from photographs.
]
