== Variance and throughput

At equal samples per pixel, NEE with MIS lowers per-pixel variance #F("nee.var_ratio", 1)× against BSDF sampling alone on a scene of two instanced boxes, with image means agreeing within noise. A gradient of the 96×96, 8 spp frame at depth 4 (#paths-96 paths, four wavelengths each) costs a median of #F("rel.grad_med", 2) ms on the GPU without NEE (#F("rel.grad_lo", 2)–#F("rel.grad_hi", 2) ms over four runs; the slowest run is the first, which includes pipeline compilation) and #F("rel.grad_nee_med", 2) ms with NEE (#F("rel.grad_nee_lo", 2)–#F("rel.grad_nee_hi", 2) ms). The forward pass alone takes a median of #F("rel.fwd_med", 2) ms, about #fx(mpaths, 0) million paths per second, so the replay and adjoint add #fx(V("rel.grad_med") - V("rel.fwd_med"), 2) ms; NEE doubles the batch. The same gradient through the CPU tier's compiled C costs a median of #fx(V("rel.cpu_grad_med"), 0) ms (#F("rel.cpu_grad_lo", 0)–#F("rel.cpu_grad_hi", 0) ms), measured in a separate test against the GPU gradient with the loss scored on the device, #fx(V("rel.gpu_grad_med"), 2) ms (median of four runs), so the GPU tier is about #fx(cpu-speedup, 0)× faster end to end on this machine. This compares irys with itself; it is not a comparison with another renderer.

== Diffuse albedo, splats and emitters <sec:ext>

The same replay differentiates two more kinds of parameter. A Lambertian BSDF is linear in its albedo, so a diffuse vertex adds $delta L thin (L - L^-) thin b_k \/ A$ to band $k$ of the albedo's gradient, with $L^-$ the radiance before the vertex. A splat's colour is linear too. Its opacity $alpha_s$ also scales everything behind it, so $partial L \/ partial alpha_s = tau T_s c_s - (E_s + R) \/ (1 - alpha_s)$, where $tau$ is the path throughput at the segment, $T_s$ the transmittance in front of splat $s$, $c_s$ its colour, $E_s$ the emission composited behind it on the segment and $R$ the light beyond the segment. On the GPU the replay writes one record per splat crossed and the host sums the records in a fixed order, so there are still no float atomics. Area emitters with next-event estimation and multiple importance sampling run on both tiers, and their #V("ext.emit.gates") gates pass on Vulkan.

